<Base title="Getting started with Saydraft — Offline dictation setup" description="Set up local speech models, choose your microphone, try a first dictation, and learn Saydraft’s Windows shortcuts.">
  <main id="main" class="document container"><p class="eyebrow">Getting started</p><h1>Your first<br /><em>few words.</em></h1>
    <h2>1. Get the app</h2><p>Start with the current instructions on the <a href={`${base}download/`}>download and setup page</a>. Saydraft runs on Windows 10 and 11.</p>
    <h2>2. Choose your microphone</h2><p>In setup’s Microphone page, keep Default Windows input or choose Refresh inputs to find a named device. Test input runs a ten-second, level-only check without transcription or saved audio. If needed, use the microphone-privacy link to allow desktop apps in Windows settings. Start in a quiet room and speak at a comfortable volume.</p>
    <h2>3. Set up your local models</h2><p>On Set up models, Check this PC offers a provisional capability recommendation. Use speech recommendation selects the suggested speech model; it does not run a performance benchmark, enable AI cleanup, or download anything.</p><p>Review the model’s download size and licenses, then choose Download / Resume. Setup shows overall progress and each file’s transfer/checksum phases, with Pause available. Installed files are checked in the background, including pinned dependencies. Later launches reuse installed models.</p><p>For Bengali or Arabic, add the optional shared All languages model. One download serves both languages; choosing a language starts no transfer.</p><p>Finish saves your microphone and model choices. The current wizard can finish before model installation is complete; you need a compatible installed speech model before dictating. Complete installation in Hub → Settings → Models if needed. Cancel preserves saved preferences; downloaded files remain separate. Shortcut learning, a dedicated practice sandbox, and the full six-step flow are still in development. See the <a href="https://github.com/tanzir71/saydraft/blob/main/docs/ONBOARDING.md">first-run implementation guide</a> for current behavior and remaining work.</p>
    <h2>4. Try a sentence</h2><p>Open an ordinary text editor and put the cursor in an editable field. Hold <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Win</kbd>, say a short sentence, and release. Your words appear after local recognition and cleanup. F9 is the default alternate shortcut; existing custom bindings are preserved.</p><p>Use Ctrl + Win + Space for hands-free dictation, Escape to cancel, and Shift + Alt + Z to paste the last saved transcript. You can rebind shortcuts in Settings.</p>
    <h2>5. Make it sound like you</h2><p>Adjust tone and intensity for each app group in Style. Add names and specialist terms to Dictionary, and turn frequently used text into Snippets. AI cleanup is optional; rules-only cleanup works without a text model.</p>
    <h2>6. Try it offline</h2><p>Finish setup, disconnect Wi-Fi, and dictate another short sentence. Speech and cleanup run on your PC. See <a href={`${base}privacy/`}>privacy and offline verification</a> for the boundaries.</p>
    <h2>If something feels wrong</h2><p>Check that the selected model is installed and the microphone meter responds. Use an ordinary, non-elevated text editor for the first test. Password and protected fields are excluded. Keep your transcript in History if an app cannot accept insertion.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/tanzir71/saydraft/issues">Report a problem on GitHub ↗</a>. Avoid including private transcripts or audio in public reports.</p>
  </main>
</Base>
